---
// The side marker on an index row: the dot(s) plus the word. Three call sites (the two index
// matrices and LockedRow), so the marker can't drift between them.
//
// `both` renders the mark's figure laid HORIZONTAL — two dots joined by a rule, 22×8 — so it
// sits on the meta line's baseline instead of climbing out of it. `design` / `build` render a
// single dot via `.side::before` in CSS. The case-study panel head draws the diagonal figure
// instead (SideMark.astro): a kicker on its own line has the height for it, a meta line
// doesn't.
//
// THE WORD IS ALWAYS EMITTED, and on the index it is clipped rather than absent — `.sw` is
// sized to nothing by `.entry .em .side .sw` in global.css, which keeps it in the
// accessibility tree and in the `title` below. Eight rows repeating "Design + Build" was the
// loudest thing in a column meant to annotate them (chooser: andrewshiau-panel-marks, Q1/05),
// but colour is never the only signal — navy and olive are 2.14:1 apart in greyscale
// (STYLE.md) — so the word is hidden from the eye and from nothing else. A `span`, not a bare
// text node, because you cannot address a text node from CSS.
//
// `title` on the wrapper, so a sighted reader who wants the word can hover for it. It is the
// one affordance replacing what the visible word did.
import { SIDE_LABEL, type Side } from '../data/experiments';
interface Props { side: Side }
const { side } = Astro.props;
---
<span class={`side side-${side}`} title={SIDE_LABEL[side]}>
  {side === 'both' && (
    <svg class="mk" viewBox="0 0 22 8" aria-hidden="true" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
      <line x1="4" y1="4" x2="18" y2="4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25"/>
      <circle cx="4" cy="4" r="4" fill="var(--design)"/>
      <circle cx="18" cy="4" r="4" fill="var(--build)"/>
    </svg>
  )}
  <span class="sw">{SIDE_LABEL[side]}</span>
</span>
